#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nf(1)=k\n$$\n\n, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 548 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
